import{_ as t,o,c as s,ag as a}from"./chunks/framework.D1z9wg1y.js";const f=JSON.parse('{"title":"The srt settings of the CLI frontends","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"components/sandbox.md","filePath":"components/sandbox.md"}'),d={name:"components/sandbox.md"};function n(i,e,l,c,r,h){return o(),s("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[a(`<h1 id="the-srt-settings-of-the-cli-frontends" tabindex="-1">The srt settings of the CLI frontends <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-srt-settings-of-the-cli-frontends" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The srt settings of the CLI frontends&quot;">​</a></h1><p><code>srt</code>, the sandbox runtime, runs one process and everything that it starts inside one sandbox. It reads one settings file. <code>harness install</code> writes one such file for each CLI frontend that the profile&#39;s <code>[setup]</code> chooses:</p><div class="language-text vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">text</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>~/.config/research-harness/sandbox/claude.json</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>~/.config/research-harness/sandbox/opencode.json</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>~/.config/research-harness/sandbox/omp.json</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>~/.config/research-harness/sandbox/antigravity.json</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The launcher <code>agentbox</code> starts a frontend under <code>srt</code> with its file, as <a href="#the-launcher-agentbox">the launcher</a> describes. A frontend that starts without it does not read the file.</p><p>The files come from two sources. The <code>sandbox</code> section of the Claude Code settings template, <code>settings/settings.proposal.json</code>, stays the source of the lists that every frontend shares: the write paths, the write and read denies, the credential files and the network rules, with the domains that <code>settings/network.toml</code> and the profile&#39;s overlay give. The policy file <code>sandbox/policy.toml</code> holds only what <code>srt</code> adds to them. <code>lib/harness/sandbox.py</code> renders the files from both.</p><h2 id="the-policy-file" tabindex="-1">The policy file <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-policy-file" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The policy file&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>sandbox/policy.toml</code> has a table <code>[read]</code> and a table <code>[frontend.&lt;name&gt;]</code> for each of <code>claude</code>, <code>opencode</code>, <code>omp</code> and <code>antigravity</code>. Every path starts with <code>~/</code>.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">key</th><th style="text-align:left;">what it holds</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>read.allow</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the paths that every frontend reads under the read limit</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>frontend.&lt;name&gt;.state</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the frontend&#39;s own directories and files, which it reads and writes</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>frontend.&lt;name&gt;.deny_write</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">write denies that the frontend adds to the template&#39;s</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>frontend.&lt;name&gt;.own</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the template&#39;s deny entries that the frontend reads and writes itself</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The read limit denies reads of the whole home directory and allows the paths of <code>read.allow</code> again. A path that the template denies stays hidden inside an allowed path: in <code>srt</code> a deny inside an allowed path wins. Claude Code&#39;s <code>own</code> is <code>~/.claude.json</code>, which Claude Code writes itself; a write deny of it would stop the whole process under <code>srt</code>. The cost is that a command that Claude Code runs can also write the file, because the sandbox of the whole process cannot tell the frontend from its own shell. oh-my-pi adds the write denies <code>~/.omp/agent/config.yml</code> and <code>~/.omp/agent/extensions</code>.</p><p><code>harness install</code> exits with status 2 on a policy file with an unknown key, a missing key or frontend, a value that is not a list of strings, a path that does not start with <code>~/</code>, or a path with a <code>..</code> segment, which could leave the home directory. The message names the key or the path.</p><h2 id="the-rendering-rules" tabindex="-1">The rendering rules <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-rendering-rules" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The rendering rules&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>srt_settings</code> gives one frontend&#39;s object, with the keys <code>network</code> and <code>filesystem</code> and no other:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">key</th><th style="text-align:left;">value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>network.allowedDomains</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the template&#39;s, in order</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>network.deniedDomains</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the template&#39;s, or <code>[]</code> where it has none; <code>srt</code> refuses a file without the key</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>network.allowLocalBinding</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the template&#39;s</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>filesystem.allowWrite</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the template&#39;s, without each entry at or below another frontend&#39;s <code>state</code> path; then the frontend&#39;s <code>state</code>; then the real path of <code>$TMPDIR</code>, where it is set and not empty</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>filesystem.denyWrite</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the template&#39;s, without the frontend&#39;s <code>own</code>; then its <code>deny_write</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>filesystem.denyRead</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the home directory; then the template&#39;s; then each path of <code>credentials.files</code>; without the frontend&#39;s <code>own</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>filesystem.allowRead</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>read.allow</code>; then the frontend&#39;s <code>state</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Each <code>~</code> and <code>~/</code> prefix becomes the home directory, so the files hold absolute paths. A list holds no entry twice, and the first occurrence keeps its place. &quot;At or below&quot; compares whole path components: <code>~/.claude.json</code> is not below <code>~/.claude</code>. So no frontend&#39;s file allows a write to another frontend&#39;s state. A glob of the template, such as <code>~/Research/**/.git/hooks</code>, passes through after the expansion. The template&#39;s <code>excludedCommands</code> is not carried: under <code>srt</code> the whole process is inside the sandbox, so no command can run outside it. The masked credentials of <code>srt</code> are not used.</p><h2 id="the-install" tabindex="-1">The install <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-install" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The install&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>harness install</code> renders the Claude Code settings template as it does for <code>~/.claude/settings.json</code>, with the profile and the network overlay, and gives its <code>sandbox</code> object to <code>srt_settings</code>. It writes each file as JSON indented by two spaces, with the keys in the order above and a final newline. The files belong to no frontend&#39;s plan: a change of them prints no restart line. A frontend that <code>[setup]</code> does not choose gets no file, and with no frontend chosen the install renders no file. The install does not change what it writes to <code>~/.claude/settings.json</code>. Because it renders the template with the network overlay for every chosen frontend, a broken overlay stops the install with status 2 also when <code>claude</code> is not chosen, for example an <code>allow</code> entry that is a URL.</p><p>With at least one frontend chosen, the install also copies the launcher <code>sandbox/agentbox</code> to <code>~/.local/bin/agentbox</code> with mode 755, a directory that a session cannot write. With <code>claude</code> chosen it writes Claude Code&#39;s override beside the settings files:</p><div class="language-text vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">text</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>~/.config/research-harness/sandbox/claude-inner-off.json   {&quot;sandbox&quot;: {&quot;enabled&quot;: false}}</span></span></code></pre></div><p>A launcher whose mode differs from 755 is a change, as a file whose bytes differ is.</p><h2 id="the-launcher-agentbox" tabindex="-1">The launcher agentbox <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-launcher-agentbox" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The launcher agentbox&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>agentbox</code> is a POSIX <code>sh</code> script that starts one CLI frontend under <code>srt</code>:</p><div class="language-sh vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">sh</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">agentbox</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> &lt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">fronten</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">d</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">&gt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> [args...]</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The frontends are the commands <code>claude</code>, <code>opencode</code>, <code>omp</code> and <code>agy</code>. <code>agentbox</code> runs</p><div class="language-sh vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">sh</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">~</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">/.local/bin/srt --settings </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">~</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">/.config/research-harness/sandbox/</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">&lt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">policy name</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">&gt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">.json -- </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">\\</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">    &lt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">program</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">&gt; </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">&lt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">extr</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">a</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">&gt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> &lt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">args..</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">.</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">&gt;</span></span></code></pre></div><p>with <code>exec</code>, so the exit status of <code>srt</code> is its own. The policy name is <code>antigravity</code> for <code>agy</code> and the frontend&#39;s name otherwise. <code>&lt;program&gt;</code> is the path that <code>command -v</code> gives on the caller&#39;s <code>PATH</code>, and the caller&#39;s arguments follow <code>&lt;extra&gt;</code>, each passed as one argument, unchanged. The <code>--</code> ends the options of <code>srt</code>, which otherwise reads a <code>--settings</code> of the frontend as its own.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">frontend</th><th style="text-align:left;"><code>&lt;extra&gt;</code></th><th style="text-align:left;">why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>claude</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>--settings ~/.config/research-harness/sandbox/claude-inner-off.json</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">Claude Code&#39;s own sandbox fails inside the sandbox of <code>srt</code>, so the override turns it off</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>opencode</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>--standalone</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">OpenCode runs its server as its own child inside the sandbox, not in a background service that a run outside the launcher could have started</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>omp</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>--approval-mode yolo</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">oh-my-pi runs its tools without a prompt; the sandbox of <code>srt</code> is the boundary</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>agy</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">nothing</td><td style="text-align:left;"></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The frontend gets <code>AGENTBOX=1</code> in its environment; every other variable stays the caller&#39;s. <code>agentbox</code> runs only the <code>srt</code> in <code>~/.local/bin</code>, never one found on the <code>PATH</code>. So it starts nothing before <code>harness setup</code> has installed <code>srt</code> in <code>~/.local/bin</code>.</p><p><code>agentbox</code> exits with status 2, starts nothing and prints one line on stderr when:</p><ul><li>no frontend is named, or the name is not one of the four; the line lists the four;</li><li><code>AGENTBOX</code> is set, so it runs inside a launcher already;</li><li><code>~/.local/bin/srt</code> is not an executable file; the line names <code>harness setup</code>;</li><li>the frontend&#39;s settings file, or for <code>claude</code> the override, is not a file; the line names the file and <code>harness install --apply</code>;</li><li><code>command -v</code> finds no program of the frontend&#39;s name;</li><li>the frontend is <code>claude</code> and an argument is <code>--settings</code> or starts with <code>--settings=</code>: Claude Code keeps only the last <code>--settings</code>, so the caller&#39;s would replace the override and turn Claude Code&#39;s own sandbox on again. More settings go in the user settings instead.</li></ul><p><code>agentbox -h</code> and <code>agentbox --help</code> print the usage and exit 0.</p><h2 id="the-pinned-srt" tabindex="-1">The pinned srt <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-pinned-srt" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The pinned srt&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>harness setup</code> installs <code>~/.local/bin/srt</code>, the <code>srt</code> that <code>agentbox</code> runs, with any frontend chosen, from two files of the harness:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">file</th><th style="text-align:left;">what it holds</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>sandbox/srt/package.json</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">a private package with one dependency, <code>@anthropic-ai/sandbox-runtime</code> at exactly 0.0.81, and the override of <code>node-forge</code> to exactly 1.4.1</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>sandbox/srt/package-lock.json</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the tree that npm resolves from that file: srt 0.0.81, <code>node-forge</code> 1.4.1, <code>zod</code>, <code>commander</code> and <code>@pondwader/socks5-server</code>, each with its <code>resolved</code> URL on <code>registry.npmjs.org</code> and its sha512 <code>integrity</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The srt package ships no lockfile of its own, so a pin of srt alone would leave its four dependencies to the registry on each install. <code>npm ci</code> installs exactly the tree of the lockfile, refuses a package whose bytes do not match its sha512, and fails on a lockfile whose dependencies do not match those of <code>package.json</code>; it does not compare the override with the lockfile. The override keeps <code>node-forge</code> at 1.4.1: srt asks for <code>^1.4.0</code>, and 1.4.0 has eleven advisories that 1.4.1 fixes.</p><p>The step copies both files into a scratch directory below <code>~/.local/share/research-harness/</code>, beside <code>srt/</code>, and runs <code>npm ci --ignore-scripts --no-audit --no-fund</code> there with the <code>npm</code> of the <code>PATH</code>, so no install script of a package runs. After npm exits 0 and has made <code>node_modules/.bin/srt</code>, the step moves the directory to <code>~/.local/share/research-harness/srt/0.0.81/</code>, links <code>~/.local/bin/srt</code> to <code>~/.local/share/research-harness/srt/0.0.81/node_modules/.bin/srt</code>, and removes the directories of other versions and the links below <code>srt/</code>. A file at <code>~/.local/bin/srt</code> is replaced by the link. The settings template allows no write at or above <code>~/.local/share/research-harness</code> and denies writes to <code>~/.local/bin</code>, so a Claude Code session can change neither the tree nor the link.</p><p>The step fails, and writes nothing below <code>srt/</code> and nothing in <code>~/.local/bin</code>, when <code>npm</code> is not on the <code>PATH</code>, when <code>node --version</code> is below 22.12.0, the <code>engines.node</code> of srt 0.0.81 (<code>npm ci</code> only warns there), when npm exits non-zero, or when <code>~/.local/bin/srt</code> is a directory. For a failed npm, the line holds the cause from npm&#39;s stderr: the text of its <code>npm error code</code> line and its first <code>npm error</code> line of other text, such as <code>code ENOTFOUND; network request to https://registry.npmjs.org/… failed</code>. It removes its scratch directory in every case. A plan finds nothing to do, and has no such step, when <code>srt/0.0.81/package-lock.json</code> holds the bytes of the shipped lockfile, <code>node_modules/.bin/srt</code> exists, and <code>~/.local/bin/srt</code> links to it; so a change of the shipped lockfile installs again. The Homebrew copy of srt, where one is installed, stays as it is.</p>`,36)])])}const g=t(d,[["render",n]]);export{f as __pageData,g as default};
